---
import BaseLayout from "../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import DebtLab from "../../components/DebtLab.tsx";
import NextTool from "../../components/NextTool.astro";
import Sources from "../../components/Sources.astro";
import "../../styles/simtool.css";
---

<BaseLayout
  title="The Cost of Debt"
  description="Compounding in reverse: at a credit card's APR, paying only the minimum stretches a small balance into decades and doubles it in interest. See the trap — and how a fixed payment breaks it."
>
  <section class="container wl-intro">
    <p class="eyebrow">Interactive Tool</p>
    <h1>The cost of debt</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      Compounding is the most powerful force in investing — and it runs in both
      directions. On a credit card, it works <em>against</em> you: pay only the
      minimum and a modest balance can take decades to clear and cost more in
      interest than you ever borrowed. This is the Compound Growth Explorer's dark
      twin — and the reason paying off high-rate debt is the surest "return" in all
      of personal finance.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="container wl-host">
    <DebtLab client:load />
  </section>

  <section class="container wl-explainer">
    <div class="prose">
      <h2>Why the minimum payment is a trap</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>The minimum shrinks as you pay.</strong> A typical minimum is the
          month's interest plus about 1% of the balance. As the balance falls, so
          does the payment — so you keep paying mostly interest, and the principal
          barely moves. That's what stretches a small balance across decades.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>A fixed payment breaks it.</strong> Pay the same dollar amount
          every month instead, and every month more of it goes to principal as the
          interest portion shrinks. The balance falls faster and faster — the same
          compounding that traps you now works for you.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Paying off debt is a guaranteed return.</strong> Clearing a 20%
          card is a risk-free, tax-free 20% return — better than the stock market's
          ~10% long-run <em>average</em>, and with none of the uncertainty. Whenever
          a debt's rate is higher than what you could reasonably earn investing,
          paying it down is mathematically the best use of the next dollar.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Not all debt is a trap — a low-rate mortgage or student loan can be
        reasonable to carry while you invest. The rate is what matters: the higher
        it is, the more certain the case for paying it off first. Educational only,
        not advice.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <Sources tool="debt" />

  <NextTool current="/tools/debt" />
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .wl-intro {
    padding-block: var(--space-lg) var(--space-md);
    max-width: var(--measure);
  }
  .lede {
    font-size: var(--step-1);
    color: var(--color-text-soft);
    line-height: 1.5;
  }
  .wl-explainer {
    margin-top: var(--space-lg);
  }
</style>

<style is:global>
  .ss-headline {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: 0.15rem;
    padding: var(--space-sm) var(--space-md);
    border-radius: var(--radius);
    background: var(--color-accent-soft);
    border: 1px solid var(--color-accent);
  }
  .ss-headline-label {
    font-family: var(--font-sans);
    font-size: var(--step--1);
    color: var(--color-text-soft);
  }
  .ss-headline-value {
    font-family: var(--font-sans);
    font-weight: 700;
    font-size: var(--step-3);
    line-height: 1.1;
    color: var(--color-accent);
    font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums;
  }
  .ss-headline-sub {
    font-family: var(--font-sans);
    font-size: var(--step--1);
    color: var(--color-text-soft);
  }
  .ss-stats {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr;
    gap: var(--space-sm);
    margin: 0 0 var(--space-md);
  }
  .ss-stats > div {
    background: var(--color-surface-alt);
    border-radius: var(--radius);
    padding: var(--space-sm);
  }
  .ss-stats dt {
    font-family: var(--font-sans);
    font-size: var(--step--1);
    color: var(--color-muted);
    margin-bottom: 0.2rem;
  }
  .ss-stats dd {
    margin: 0;
    font-family: var(--font-sans);
    font-weight: 700;
    font-size: var(--step-0);
    font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums;
  }
</style>
